ns based in  part on the aftermath of SARS where more  than 40% of Canadians suffered from  increased stress levels but by the time  this Panda  is over numbers could climb even higher  separate polling by both if Sousa and  Angus Reid show half of Canadians feel  their mental health has already worsened  we know that it's not it's not just a  secondary wave it's actually happening  right now our service volume has doubled  for young people who are reaching out  for support an increase of about 50%  more calls than we were receiving this  time last year two examples from  organizations on the front lines  typically what we would see a social  isolation increased concerns around  mental health or symptoms that they're  experiencing and what we're seeing with  kovat is an increase in all of those  things that they've been somehow  amplified it's also meant a change in  how mental health workers provide that  desperately needed support  Toronto's Gerstein Center was forced to  close their short-term beds and park  their mobile crisis van now they're  focused on calls and wellness check  outreach but it's not the same as soon  as we can get back to face to face the  better these staff take calls for 12  hours a day and in a pandemic that can  feel endless the mental health of  frontline workers is also a concern  there's still this lingering concern of  going into work and working in close  contact with people and potentially  contacting iris Courtney Jackson just  launched the frontlines a publication on  medium encouraging other workers to  share their stories she's a mental  health case manager at a residential  care facility in Ontario burnout is a  real problem  so is isolation with many health care  workers self isolating from loved ones  within their own homes I mean working in  the mental health fields you expect to  deal with stress and change it's it's  part of the job but the difference is  that right now I'm working with people  who have a limited understanding of the  situation they feel powerless to the  situation but I myself also feel that  way so in a country that saw long wait  times for mental health services before  the pandemic struck what's being done to  help health care is a prevention  responsibilities and a number of  provinces have stepped up with new  programs and millions in new funding  help is available Alberta is leading the  way after a government survey found 74  percent of Albertans feel their mental  health has been hit by kovat 19 the 53  million dollar additional investment  that we're announcing today is more than  all of the other provinces but what's  being done at the federal level the  Prime Minister keeps promising all kinds  of help but the fact is there's no clear  number on new mental health dollars  coming from Ottawa as the funding is  tied to other things this month the  Prime Minister committed two hundred and  forty million dollars to boost access to  virtual care including mental health and  25 million of that is going to the new  wellness together portal connecting  Canadians with free help including phone  text and video counseling there will be  more to do and we know that this the  secondary pandemic if you will is just  as in many ways as in my fact I would  say it's just as urgent as this one in  its first three weeks more than a  hundred and fifty thousand Canadians  used the portal help is offered via a  number of partner agencies not  government employees after eight  thousand volunteers answered a call from  Kids Help Phone now the service known  for helping kids is responsible for all  the texts that come through the portal  anybody that texts whether they're a  youth or they're an adult will text 24/7  and reach one of our trained volunteer  crisis responders and those are  volunteers that go through a rigorous  training process and they're overseen in  real time by paid professional staff but  the site does ask you to provide your  name and email the agency overseeing it  says there is help available without  registering and the data is only visible  to the person submitting it designed to  be like a Fitbit people are able to see  their progress so that's the Peter  Cornish says they're working on a way to  make registration optional as they brace  for increased demand over the next few  months he says as it develops peer  mentor  will become more important so that  everyone can support each other better  which is why we came up with the name  wellness together Canada Cristina del  corpo is managing pretty well these days  and she wants to help others too so  she's been blogging you didn't always  feel this stressed out this bad and it's  gonna pass and just keep that in mind  and keep pushing for it and if you  really need help reach out to someone  the battlecry of this pandemic has been  we're all in this together or put  another way you're not alone  [Music]
 

